  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 223 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A large, closed upper low over Hudson Bay in Canada will \nsend a prefrontal trough shortwave through northern New York and \nVermont this afternoon, arriving into a warm, humid air mass with \ntemperatures in the upper 70s to mid 80s. This will result in likely \nshowers and thunderstorms over the next several hours, most \nwidespread across the Champlain Valley and northern Vermont. Severe \nweather is not expected from these storms with minimal shear \navailable, though any shower or storm could produce heavy rainfall \nwith precipitable water values 1.50-1.75 inches and slow storm \nmotions. Locations that have received heavy rainfall already in the \npast 24 hours will be particularly at risk for flooding and ponding \nof water in low lying spots. Precipitation should wane overnight \ntonight, but chances remain across northern New York and northern \nVermont for additional heavy showers and embedded rumbles of thunder \nas a cold front moves through the region (20-60% precip chances). \nLows tonight will remain mild ahead of the front in the upper 50s \nand 60s. A brief break in the unsettled pattern is expected to \ndevelop throughout the day Sunday as a narrow axis of high pressure \nand drier air move over the region, though high temperatures will \nremain warm in the upper 70s to mid 80s and there is the chance of a \nshower or two with embedded rumbles of thunder (20-50% chance \nprecip) on northern and western slopes of the Adirondacks as well as \nnorthern Vermont along the international border. Sunday night, \ntemperatures should fall back towards seasonal normals in the 50s to \nmid 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: We remain in the cyclonic flow of the closed upper low \nover Hudson Bay in Canada early next week, and another shortwave is \nexpected to drive through northern New York and Vermont Monday and \nMonday night. This will likely produce precipitation across northern \nNew York Monday afternoon, expanding to Vermont during the \nevening/overnight hours (50-90% chance precip). Highs both Monday \nand Tuesday will be in the mid 70s to mid 80s, resulting in the \npotential for showers and thunderstorms. Once again, wind shear is \nlooking unimpressive (limited severe threat), but moisture will \nadvect back in from the Great Lakes with modeled Pwats 1.50-1.75\" \nreturning. We'll be monitoring this period for flooding potential, \nparticularly Monday evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: The unsettled weather pattern will continue for the \nsecond half of the week.  In general, expect intervals of showers \nand thunderstorms interspersed with drier days and a trend \ntowards slightly cooler temperatures towards the weekend.\n\nThe midweek timeframe will feature progressive zonal flow that will \nsend a series of quick-moving systems through our area.  Despite the \nnumerous chances for rain, hydro concerns are low given the expected \nprogressive movement of the systems. For Wednesday and Thursday, the \nbest chances for showers (and possibly a few embedded thunderstorms) \nwill be during the afternoon hours as marginal instability develops. \n\nLooking towards the end of the week, the upper-level flow will \namplify as ridging builds over the western US with downstream \ntroughing over the eastern US/Canada. The cyclonic flow over the \nnortheastern US will favor a continuation of scattered showers \nwith slightly below normal temperatures for the weekend.",
